nak-hw-tests-pascal.patch: two NAK hw_tests fixes for Pascal, for upstream
Mesa 17ca6174. Applied by nvk/pascal/scripts/build-nak-nouveau.sh (the NAK
test binary in the "NAK on nouveau" bundle). Test code only: hw_tests.rs is
not part of libvulkan_nouveau or of the macOS dylib.

Both failed on the GP106 in run 2 (2026-11-08, 43/45 passed):

1. test_render_enable: backport of upstream 023d491a ("nak/hw_tests: Adjust
   test_render_enable for pre-Turing", Mary Guillemard; Reviewed-by: Mel
   Henning; mesa!45142), verbatim. That commit is newer than 17ca6174.
   Before Turing the copy engine's render-enable compares 32-bit values
   (CONDITIONAL: low word != 0; RENDER_IF_[NOT_]EQUAL: words at +0 and
   +16). Run 2 failed on Conditional [1,0,0,0,0], which the old 64-bit
   expectation called false. NVK's conditional rendering is fine on Pascal
   either way: it uses only RENDER_IF_[NOT_]EQUAL, writes the 32-bit
   predicate to the low word of operand A and keeps operand B (+16) zero.

2. test_qmd_dependencies: its shaders use uniform registers, OpLdcg (the
   SM70 encoder asserts sm >= 75) and NANOSLEEP (SM70+). On SM 6.1 the SM50
   encoder panicked: "Unhandled instruction nanosleep". The test now
   returns early below SM75. Upstream main still gates it at
   PASCAL_COMPUTE_A, so it has the same failure there (checked 2026-11-08).
   Dependent QMDs on Pascal stay covered by test_qmd_dependencies_simple,
   which passed on the GP106. Neither OpNanosleep nor OpLdcg is ever made
   by from_nir, so no application shader can reach the panic.

--- a/src/nouveau/compiler/nak/hw_tests.rs
+++ b/src/nouveau/compiler/nak/hw_tests.rs
@@ -1834,20 +1834,34 @@
 fn expected_render_enable(
     mode: cl90b5::SetRenderEnableCMode,
     data: &[u64],
+    sm: u8,
 ) -> bool {
     use cl90b5::SetRenderEnableCMode::*;
+
+    let data_u32: &[u32] = unsafe {
+        core::slice::from_raw_parts(data.as_ptr() as *const u32, data.len() * 2)
+    };
+
     match mode {
         True => true,
         False => false,
-        Conditional => data[0] > u32::MAX.into(),
-        RenderIfEqual => data[0] == data[2],
-        RenderIfNotEqual => data[0] != data[2],
+
+        // New generation behaviors
+        Conditional if sm >= 70 => data[0] > u32::MAX.into(),
+        RenderIfEqual if sm >= 75 => data[0] == data[2],
+        RenderIfNotEqual if sm >= 75 => data[0] != data[2],
+
+        // Old generation behaviors
+        Conditional => data_u32[0] != 0,
+        RenderIfEqual => data_u32[0] == data_u32[4],
+        RenderIfNotEqual => data_u32[0] != data_u32[4],
     }
 }
 
 #[test]
 fn test_render_enable() -> io::Result<()> {
     let run = RunSingleton::get();
+    let sm = run.sm.sm();
     let dev = run.run.device();
     let ctx = Context::new(dev.clone()).expect("Failed to create context");
 
@@ -1960,7 +1974,7 @@
     for &mode in modes {
         let check = |data: &[u64]| -> io::Result<()> {
             let actual = run(mode, data)?;
-            let expected = expected_render_enable(mode, data);
+            let expected = expected_render_enable(mode, data, sm);
             if actual != expected {
                 println!("input: {mode:?} {data:?}");
             }
@@ -2440,6 +2454,14 @@
         return Ok(());
     }
 
+    // The shaders below use uniform registers, OpLdcg (encoded from SM75 on)
+    // and NANOSLEEP (SM70 on); the SM50 encoder has neither instruction.
+    // Dependent QMDs on Pascal and Volta are covered by
+    // test_qmd_dependencies_simple.
+    if run.sm.sm() < 75 {
+        return Ok(());
+    }
+
     let shader_slow = {
         let mut b = TestShaderBuilder::new(&run.sm);
 
